  "body": "The last case the fallback audit left open.\n\n## The gap\n\nA stream that has shown the reader part of an answer **cannot** fall back — switching models would splice two answers together. But nothing bounded it either: the first-token deadline stops applying the moment content appears, so the only limit left was the whole-request budget.\n\nOn 21 days of measured traffic, **106 requests held one open for the full 600s** after producing real output.\n\n`FALLBACK_STALL_TIMEOUT`, default **120s**, `0` disables.\n\n**Measured from the last chunk that moved the answer forward** — never from the last frame of any kind. A keepalive proves the connection is alive, not that the answer is advancing, and a guard that accepted one would never fire on the streams it exists for. A model producing steadily is therefore never cut, however long its answer runs.\n\n## Both of the requested properties, demonstrated rather than argued\n\nLive, against a real server and a real upstream:\n\n```\nanswers then dies       10s, all 5 deltas delivered, clean terminal error\n                        naming the stall limit\n                        (121s before, on the provider read timeout;\n                         600s in production, on the budget)\n\n15s of steady output    16s, 60 content deltas, 0 errors, proper\nunder an 8s limit       message_stop — untouched\n```\n\n**No new failure mode.** The failure it raises is the one the total budget already raised on exactly these streams, via the same `anthropic_terminal_error_frame`. This is an earlier trigger for an existing outcome.\n\n**It cannot introduce an unbounded wait, and removes one.** With the guard on, a committed stream is bounded even when `FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT` is disabled. Proven by mutation: removing the limit makes the suite *hang*.\n\n## Choosing 120s\n\nDeliberately the same number as `FALLBACK_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT` rather than a new invented one. It answers the same question — how long may this model be silent — and mid-answer silence is if anything *more* suspicious than silence before the answer, which at least covers queueing and cold starts.\n\nFor scale: across **146,857 successful requests** the slowest averaged one output token every **2.27 seconds**, so the default sits ~50× beyond the worst rate ever observed here. An average cannot bound a single long pause, so this is a judgement rather than a proof — which is why `0` disables it and the setting is on the dashboard.\n\n## Verification\n\n| mutation | caught by |\n| --- | --- |\n| M1 no stall limit at all | suite **hangs** — the unbounded wait, shown |\n| M2 any chunk counts as progress | `keepalive_does_not_count_as_progress` |\n| M3 clock never resets | `stream_that_keeps_producing_is_never_cut` |\n| M4 `0` no longer disables it | `disabling_tolerates_a_long_pause` |\n| M5 stall misreported as the budget | `quiet_after_producing`, `keepalive` |\n\n**M2 and M4 survived a first pass** and the tests were strengthened rather than the finding dropped: M2's keepalives were emitted back-to-back so the stream tripped either way, and M4 asserted only the error message, not the behaviour. An earlier run also reported three *false* survivals caused by a `-k` filter that excluded the very tests that catch them — the probe, not the code.\n\n`test_no_limit_field_is_orphaned` caught the new field before I did; `LIMIT_KEYS` is updated.\n\nLocal CI: `ruff format --check`, `ruff check`, `ty` clean; pytest **4,836 passed** with the documented 6 `test_install_ps1_*` baseline failures.\n\n## Found while testing, NOT fixed here\n\n**The holdback buffer only checks its window when a new event arrives.** A stream that emits a burst of content and then stops never flushes — the content is discarded and the request is reported as *\"produced no output\"*, despite the model having produced some. Reproduced with a stub sending five deltas at once and stalling; it predates this change and needs a timer in the provider's read loop, which is its own PR. Say the word.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **The default is a judgement, not a proof.** If a provider legitimately pauses >120s mid-answer, its requests will now end. `FALLBACK_STALL_TIMEOUT=0` restores the old behaviour, and the setting is bounded 0–1h in the dashboard.\n- **Silent extended thinking.** If a model reasons without streaming reasoning deltas, that silence counts against the limit. In the Anthropic shape thinking precedes content, so it is normally covered by the first-token deadline instead — but interleaved thinking mid-turn is the case to watch.\n- The guard adds one substring scan per chunk. Exact for this protocol: only a `content_block_delta` can carry content.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
