    "message": "feat(routing): stop a stream that produced output and then went quiet\n\nThe last case the fallback audit left open. A stream that has shown the reader\npart of an answer cannot fall back -- switching models would splice two\nanswers together -- but nothing bounded it either. The first-token deadline\nstops applying the moment content appears, so the only limit left was the\nwhole-request budget: on 21 days of measured traffic 106 requests held one\nopen for the full 600s after producing real output.\n\nFALLBACK_STALL_TIMEOUT, default 120s, 0 disables.\n\nMeasured from the last chunk that moved the answer forward, never from the\nlast frame of any kind. A keepalive proves the connection is alive, not that\nthe answer is advancing, and a guard that accepted one would never fire on the\nstreams it exists for. A model producing steadily is therefore never cut,\nhowever long its answer runs.\n\nTwo properties, both demonstrated rather than argued\n----------------------------------------------------\nLive, against a real server and a real upstream (throwaway HOME):\n\n    answers then dies    10s, all 5 deltas delivered, clean terminal error\n                         naming the stall limit\n                         (121s before, on the provider read timeout;\n                          600s in production, on the budget)\n\n    15s of steady output 16s, 60 content deltas, 0 errors, proper\n    under an 8s limit    message_stop -- untouched\n\nThe failure it raises is the one the total budget already raised on these\nstreams, so a client sees nothing new: this is an earlier trigger for an\nexisting outcome, not a new failure mode.\n\nIt cannot introduce an unbounded wait, and removes one: with the guard on, a\ncommitted stream is bounded even when FALLBACK_TOTAL_TIMEOUT is disabled.\nVerified by mutation -- removing the limit makes the test suite hang.\n\nChoosing 120s\n-------------\nDeliberately the same number as FALLBACK_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT rather than a new\none. It answers the same question -- how long may this model be silent -- and\nmid-answer silence is if anything more suspicious than silence before the\nanswer, which at least covers queueing and cold starts.\n\nFor scale: across 146,857 successful requests the slowest averaged one output\ntoken every 2.27 seconds, so the default sits about fifty times beyond the\nworst rate ever observed here. An average cannot bound a single long pause, so\nthis is a judgement rather than a proof, which is why 0 disables it and the\nsetting is on the dashboard.\n\nVerification\n------------\nGuards mutation-tested, each failing by name:\n\n    M1 no stall limit at all        -> suite HANGS (the unbounded wait, shown)\n    M2 any chunk counts as progress -> keepalive_does_not_count_as_progress\n    M3 clock never resets           -> stream_that_keeps_producing_is_never_cut\n    M4 0 no longer disables it      -> disabling_tolerates_a_long_pause\n    M5 stall misreported as budget  -> quiet_after_producing, keepalive\n\nM2 and M4 SURVIVED a first pass and the tests were strengthened rather than the\nfinding dropped: M2's keepalives were emitted back to back so the stream\ntripped either way, and M4 asserted only the error message, not the behaviour.\nAn earlier run also reported three false survivals from a `-k` filter that\nexcluded the very tests that catch them.\n\nAlso found, not fixed\n---------------------\nThe holdback buffer only checks its window when a new event arrives, so a\nstream that emits a burst of content and then stops never flushes: the content\nis discarded and the request is reported as \"produced no output\". Reproduced\nwith a stub that sends five deltas at once and stalls. It predates this change\nand needs a timer in the provider's read loop, which is its own change.\n\nLocal CI: ruff format --check, ruff check, ty clean; pytest 4,836 passed with\nthe documented 6 test_install_ps1_* baseline failures.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
